
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Student
{
    int roll;
    char name[50];

public:

    // Default Constructor
    Student()
    {
        roll = 0;
        cout << "Default Constructor Called\n";
    }

    // Parameterized Constructor
    Student(int r, char n[])
    {
        roll = r;

        int i;
        for(i = 0; n[i] != '\0'; i++)
            name[i] = n[i];

        name[i] = '\0';

        cout << "Parameterized Constructor Called\n";
    }

    void display()
    {
        cout << "Roll Number: " << roll << endl;
        cout << "Name: " << name << endl;
    }
};

int main()
{
    Student s1;

    Student s2(101, "Rahul");

    cout << "\nDefault Constructor Output:\n";
    s1.display();

    cout << "\nParameterized Constructor Output:\n";
    s2.display();

    return 0;
}
output-
Default Constructor Called
Parameterized Constructor Called

Default Constructor Output:
Roll Number: 0
Name:

Parameterized Constructor Output:
Roll Number: 101
Name: Rahul
A constructor is a special member function that is automatically called when an object is created.
- Default Constructor: A constructor that takes no arguments.
- Parameterized Constructor: A constructor that takes arguments to initialize data members.

5 Explain Destructor with a suitable example.
Answer:

A destructor is a special member function that is automatically called when an object is destroyed. It is used to perform cleanup operations.

Features of Destructor:
1. It has the same name as the class.
2. It starts with a tilde (~) symbol.
3. It has no return type.
4. It does not take arguments.
5. It is called automatically when an object goes out of scope.

#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Student
{
public:

    Student()
    {
        cout << "Constructor Called\n";
    }

    ~Student()
    {
        cout << "Destructor Called\n";
    }
};

int main()
{
    Student s;

    cout << "Inside Main Function\n";

    return 0;
}

output-
Constructor Called
Inside Main Function
Destructor Called

6 Write a C++ program to demonstrate the types of inheritance with a suitable example.
Answer:
Inheritance is an important feature of C++ in which one class acquires the properties and functions of another class.

There are five main types of inheritance:
1. Single Inheritance
2. Multiple Inheritance
3. Multilevel Inheritance
4. Hierarchical Inheritance
5. Hybrid Inheritance


#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

// Base Class
class Student
{
protected:
    int roll;

public:
    void getRoll()
    {
        cout << "Enter Roll Number: ";
        cin >> roll;
    }
};

// Derived Class
class Result : public Student
{
    float marks;

public:
    void getMarks()
    {
        cout << "Enter Marks: ";
        cin >> marks;
    }

    void display()
    {
        cout << "\nRoll Number: " << roll;
        cout << "\nMarks: " << marks;
    }
};

int main()
{
    Result r;

    r.getRoll();
    r.getMarks();
    r.display();

    return 0;
}
output-
Enter Roll Number: 101
Enter Marks: 85

Roll Number: 101
Marks: 85

